index 0000000..a825bab
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/nested-container-builds.md
@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+# Building inside the container
+
+`make docker-app` is the documented way to manufacture an app without installing the
+factory on the host. On a checkout that has never been bootstrapped it stops five
+times before the workflow starts, and every one of those stops is a bare message
+from a different program. This is the list, in the order they arrive, with what each
+one is actually asking for.
+
+Nothing here changes a build that already works. Four of the five are runtime state
+the image deliberately does not carry, and the fifth is a configuration key the
+engine does not accept.
+
+## 1. User namespaces — the build node cannot sandbox
+
+**Says:** the build node reports *"all agent attempts produced no project files"*,
+and above it, from Codex: `bwrap: No permissions to create a new namespace`.
+
+**Is:** Docker's default seccomp profile refuses `clone(CLONE_NEWUSER)`. Codex then
+exits 0 having written nothing, which is why this reads like a model failure.
+
+**Check it:**
+
+```bash
+docker compose exec olympus unshare -Urn true && echo ok
+```
+
+**Fix:** either enable unprivileged user namespaces on the host (preferred), or run
+this one container with the profile off — see `compose.nested-userns.yml`, which
+explains the trade and does not apply itself unless you name it:
+
+```bash
+docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f compose.nested-userns.yml up -d
+```
+
+Do not read this as "run the agent unsandboxed". The sandbox is the thing that is
+missing; the fix is to give it the namespace it needs.
+
+## 2. The Archon CLI — `core-modules/` is empty in git
+
+**Says:** *"no working archon CLI found. Tried: /app/core-modules/archon/bin/archon"*.
+
+**Is:** `core-modules/` is populated by `./setup.sh`, which clones the pinned
+mirrors, and the image does not run it. Note that `bin/archon` does not exist
+anywhere in the upstream tree at the pinned SHA either — what the workflow runs is
+the released CLI:
+
+```bash
+curl -fsSL https://archon.diy/install | sh          # archon-linux-x64
+install -m 0755 archon /usr/local/bin/archon        # inside the container
+```
+
+## 3. `uv` — every greenfield node needs it
+
+**Says:** *"the 'uv' runtime is missing, and every archon-greenfield node needs it"*.
+
+The workflow resolves `uv` from `PATH`, and a container's `PATH` is not the
+operator's — so install it where every account can execute it:
+
+```bash
+curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | UV_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh
+```
+
+## 4. A git repository
+
+**Says:** *"Error: Not in a git repository. The Archon CLI must be run from within a
+git repository."*
+
+**Is:** `.dockerignore` excludes `.git`, so `/app` is not one, and the engine
+captures the source tree through git:
+
+```bash
+git -C /app init -q && git -C /app -c user.email=build@localhost \
+  -c user.name=build commit -q --allow-empty -m baseline
+```
+
+## 5. `.archon/config.yaml` — a real configuration defect
+
+**Says:** *"Invalid assistants config in '/app/.archon/config.yaml':
+'assistants.codex.apiBaseUrl': unknown provider setting"*.
+
+**Is:** the committed config writes `apiBaseUrl` and `defaultModel` for both
+assistants. The engine's codex run-config schema takes `model`,
+`modelReasoningEffort`, `webSearchMode`, `additionalDirectories` and
+`codexBinaryPath` — nothing else — and refuses an unknown key outright. So as
+committed, a checkout cannot run its own workflow on the engine it pins. The gateway
+does not need the key: `build-app.py` writes the run's own `CODEX_HOME` config from
+`OMNIROUTE_BASE_URL`/`OMNIROUTE_API_KEY`. Remove `apiBaseUrl` and rename
+`defaultModel` to `model`.
+
+## One line that is noise
+
+Archon's title generator logs *"Claude Code SDK does not support bypassPermissions
+when running as root (UID 0)"* and falls back. The conversation title is cosmetic
+and the workflow proceeds; `IS_SANDBOX=1` in the environment silences it.
+
+## Then, and this is the part worth knowing
+
+Clearing those five gets the DAG running — `[load]`, `[plan]`, `[build]`,
+`[verify]`, `[record]` — and its output does **not** land in this checkout. `builds/`
+is a named volume (`olympus-builds:/app/builds`), so the manufactured app is inside
+the container while `app-package`, `site-package`, `app-up` and `apps-list` all read
+`./builds/<slug>` on the host: the run reports `8 files, 10143 bytes` and the very
+next command answers *"no build at builds/<slug> — package it first"*. `make
+docker-app` copies the tree out of the volume when the run succeeds, which is the
+line that makes the rest of the pipeline reachable.
+
+## The last one is not about the container at all
+
+A plan can name a `run.start` that the image `runtime.language` selects cannot
+execute. The node image has `python3` in the *build* stage (it is installed for
+native modules) and the published stage is a fresh base plus `COPY --from=build`, so
+a project whose own `start` script shells out to `python3` builds, starts, and dies
+on its healthcheck six times with `sh: python3: not found`.
+
+The plan to read for this is the plan's own shape: if there is nothing to install
+and nothing to build, the artifact is files, and `static` is the language that says
+so — nginx serves them and the plan's `start` is deliberately not run. `static` is
+refused together with an `install` or `build` command, so the plan has to be
+consistent, not merely relabelled.
